/** @page odbcschemaguide Schema and Result Metadata

    @tableofcontents

    The ODBC driver exposes result-column metadata through the standard %Qore DBI APIs.
    Use @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::describe() for a prepared statement. It returns column
    descriptions; it does not enumerate a database's tables, keys, indexes, or stored procedures.
    The driver implements statement-level description only; the separate
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::describe() method is not supported.

    To inspect a database schema, query the database's system catalog using
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRows(). Catalog views and the information they expose
    depend on the database server. The module does not expose standalone ODBC catalog functions.

    @section odbc_schema_columns Describing query columns

    @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::describe() returns a hash keyed by result-column name. Each
    value describes that column's name, %Qore type code, maximum size, native type, and native
    type code. The ODBC driver also reports column nullability. See the API reference for the
    return format and @ref odbcbindguide for SQL-to-%Qore type mappings.

    This example assumes \c ds is an open datasource and the database contains a \c users table
    with \c id and \c name columns. The false predicate avoids fetching table contents while
    retaining the result-column descriptions:

    Obtain a statement with @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::getSQLStatement(), set its SQL with
    @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::prepare(), execute it with @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::exec(),
    and read the column descriptions with @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::describe(). Close the
    statement with @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::close() when it is no longer needed.

    @code{.py}
SQLStatement stmt = ds.getSQLStatement();
on_exit stmt.close();
stmt.prepare("SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE 1 = 0");
stmt.exec();
hash<auto> columns = stmt.describe();
printf("%Y\n", columns);
    @endcode

    The surrounding transaction remains the caller's responsibility; see @ref odbctrans and
    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::commit() / @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::rollback().

    @section odbc_schema_tables Querying the database catalog

    @anchor odbc_schema_primary_keys
    @anchor odbc_schema_foreign_keys
    @anchor odbc_schema_indexes
    @anchor odbc_schema_procedures
    @anchor odbc_schema_type_info

    @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRows() returns query results as a list of row hashes,
    or \c NOTHING when no rows match. The following PostgreSQL example lists tables visible
    through its \c information_schema.tables view. Adapt catalog queries to the connected
    database; ODBC does not make a server's SQL catalog portable.

    @code{.py}
*list<auto> tables = ds.selectRows("SELECT table_schema, table_name FROM information_schema.tables "
    "WHERE table_schema = %v ORDER BY table_name", "public");
printf("%Y\n", tables);
    @endcode

    Primary and foreign keys, indexes, stored procedures, and available data types likewise
    require the appropriate catalog queries for the database server. Result-column descriptions
    alone do not provide that schema information.
*/
